    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">
      Whole-House Fan CFM Sizing &amp; Attic Vent Area Ratio Guide
    </h1>
    <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
      A whole-house fan is one of the most cost-effective mechanical cooling investments in residential construction. However, you cannot simply buy the largest fan available at a home center. A whole-house fan operates as a high-volume positive displacement pump: all the air sucked out of your living space must escape through your attic vents. Understanding the <strong>CFM-to-attic vent area ratio</strong> is critical to preventing motor burnout, attic pressurization, and carbon monoxide hazards.
    </p>

    <!-- Key Metrics Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-4 p-4 bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 mb-6">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 font-medium">Cooling Air Changes</div>
        <div class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900">20 – 30 ACH</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-blue-600">Structural Thermal Purge</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 font-medium">Attic Exhaust Ratio</div>
        <div class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900">1 Sq Ft / 750 CFM</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-emerald-600">Net Free Vent Area (NFVA)</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 font-medium">Insect Screen Loss</div>
        <div class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900">50% Restriction</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-purple-600">1/8" Mesh Screen Factor</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 font-medium">Quick CFM Rule</div>
        <div class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900">2.0 to 2.5 x Sq Ft</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-amber-600">Floor Area Sizing</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Content Body -->
  <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6 mb-8">
    <h2>1. The Air Changes per Hour (ACH) Sizing Formula</h2>
    <p>
      Unlike central air conditioning systems that recirculate and chill indoor air, a whole-house fan cools by rapidly flushing the entire volume of warm indoor air and replacing it with cooler outdoor night air. To cool not just the air, but the thermal mass of your framing, drywall, and furniture, building science dictates <strong>20 to 30 air changes per hour (ACH)</strong>:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-600 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-amber-900 my-4">
      Required CFM = (Total Living Area Sq Ft x Ceiling Height x Target ACH) / 60 Minutes
    </div>
    <p>
      For a 2,200 square foot single-family home with standard 8-foot ceilings targeting 25 ACH:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-gray-100 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-gray-800 my-2">
      • Total Cubic Feet = 2,200 sq ft x 8 ft = 17,600 cubic feet<br/>
      • Required CFM = (17,600 x 25) / 60 = 440,000 / 60 = <strong>7,333 CFM (or ~4,500 to 5,000 CFM for zoned bedroom cooling)</strong>
    </div>

    <h2>2. The 1 Sq Ft per 750 CFM Attic Exhaust Rule</h2>
    <p>
      This is where most DIY installations fail. A fan pulling 4,500 CFM forces 4,500 cubic feet of air into your attic every minute. If your attic roof vents are too small, that air cannot escape. Pressure builds up inside the attic, creating severe aerodynamic back-pressure that cuts the fan's actual airflow in half and overheats the fan motor.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Home Ventilating Institute (HVI) and International Residential Code establish the standard ratio:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-600 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-blue-900 my-4">
      Minimum Net Free Vent Area (NFVA in Sq Ft) = Fan CFM / 750
    </div>
    <p>
      For a 4,500 CFM fan:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-gray-100 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-gray-800 my-2">
      NFVA = 4,500 / 750 = <strong>6.0 Square Feet of Net Free Vent Area</strong>
    </div>

    <h2>3. Net Free Vent Area (NFVA) Sizing Table</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-700 uppercase font-semibold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Fan CFM Rating</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Recommended Home Size</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Required Net Free Vent Area</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Gross Louver Vent Area Needed (50% Mesh)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 bg-white">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">2,500 CFM</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Up to 1,200 Sq Ft</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-blue-700 font-bold">3.33 Sq Ft</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">6.7 Sq Ft Gross (e.g., two 20x24 gable louvers)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">3,500 CFM</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">1,200 – 1,700 Sq Ft</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-blue-700 font-bold">4.67 Sq Ft</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">9.3 Sq Ft Gross</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">4,500 CFM</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">1,700 – 2,400 Sq Ft</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">6.00 Sq Ft</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">12.0 Sq Ft Gross</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">5,500 CFM</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">2,400 – 3,000 Sq Ft</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">7.33 Sq Ft</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">14.7 Sq Ft Gross</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">6,500 CFM</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-purple-700 font-bold">3,000+ Sq Ft</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-purple-700 font-bold">8.67 Sq Ft</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-purple-700 font-bold">17.3 Sq Ft Gross</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>4. Accounting for Vent Screen Obstruction Factors</h2>
    <p>
      A common misconception is measuring the physical dimensions of a gable louver (e.g. 24" x 24" = 4.0 sq ft) and assuming it provides 4 square feet of vent area. In reality, aluminum angled slats and insect screening block a massive percentage of the opening:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>1/4" Hardware Cloth (Bird Screen):</strong> 80% free area (Gross Area x 0.80 = Net Free Area).</li>
      <li><strong>1/8" Fine Insect Wire Mesh:</strong> 50% free area (Gross Area x 0.50 = Net Free Area).</li>
      <li><strong>Metal Louvers with 1/8" Insect Mesh:</strong> 40% to 50% free area. A 24"x24" gable vent actually delivers only <strong>1.6 to 2.0 square feet</strong> of true net free ventilation!</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Always calculate your attic exhaust based on the manufacturer-stamped <strong>NFVA rating</strong>, or cut additional gable louvers, dormer roof vents, or continuous ridge vents to meet the 1 sq ft per 750 CFM rule.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Trade Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    expertName="Marcus Vance"
    role="Master Electrician & MEP Systems Specialist"
    experience="22+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-amber-100 text-amber-800"
    initials="MV"
    title="Why You Should Never Count Soffit Vents as Exhaust for a Whole-House Fan"
    note="When calculating attic net free exhaust area, homeowners frequently add up their continuous perforated soffit vents to meet the 6.0 sq ft requirement. That is a major mistake! Soffit vents are INTAKE vents located at the lowest eaves of the roof. When a whole-house fan discharges 4,500 CFM into the center of the attic, air naturally rushes upward toward the high ridge and gable vents. If the attic lacks high exhaust vents, the air gets forced DOWNWARD through the soffit vents. As air blasts backward out the soffits, it catches outdoor eaves, swirls around, and gets sucked right back into the open first-floor windows below! You end up recirculating dusty, 130°F attic air straight into your bedrooms. For whole-house fan ventilation, only count HIGH exhaust vents: ridge vents, gable louvers, and roof-deck dormer vents located well above the eaves."
  />

